        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Using mini.completion with snippet support the issue does not occur.\r\n\r\nYeah, I specifically tested against all use cases from the similar issues.\r\n\r\n> I wonder if there is a lesson to be learned here, a better approach than `resubscribe`.\r\n\r\nMy *guess* as to why this happened remains the same. Both 'nvim-cmp' and 'blink.cmp' probably do the following sequence after completion item is accepted:\r\n- Send `completionItem/resolve` request to LSP server(s) to resolve the item for possibly missed earlier `additionalTextEdits`.\r\n- Remove currently placed word and start snippet session.\r\n- After some (small) time, callback from LSP requests act on the text. But the problem is that they act on the version of text that was *before* starting snippet session, which can cause issue.\r\n\r\n'mini.completion' does it a bit differently:\r\n- Apply already present `additionalTextEdits` with [the following caveat](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/0a9259e95dbfcd07d5d506c56afd615e018acc4e/doc/mini-completion.txt#L117-L119). Do so while tracking possible changes to currently inserted word range (for the next step).\r\n- Remove currently inserted word and insert snippet.\r\n\r\nThe solution here might be to check for `vim.b.changedtick` in the `completionItem/resolve` LSP callback: if it increased, do nothing. Not sure how well this goes with respect to usability, though.",
